  "summary": "The same color, two ways: Albers proved color only exists in relation.",
  "description": "Josef Albers built the 20th century's color pedagogy around one demonstration: identical colors look different depending on their neighbors. His color courses (Bauhaus, Black Mountain College, Yale) ran students through decades of cut-paper experiments — make one color look like two, make two look like one — and culminated in the 1963 Interaction of Color and the Homage to the Square series (1950-76), a thousand nested squares where inner \"glow\" is pure perceptual effect. The lessons — simultaneous contrast, vibrating boundaries, transparency illusions — are baked into every design curriculum and palette tool; \"Albers exercise\" remains a working verb among colorists.",
  "culturalContext": "Albers' color pedagogy is design education's backbone: Interaction of Color and the Homage to the Square turned perception experiments into a curriculum taught worldwide, and \"the Albers exercise\" remains a working verb among painters, textile designers and digital colorists.",
